---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Bouldering" nav="life" description="No ropes, no harness — just you, the wall, and a problem to solve.">
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <a href="/#life" class="back">← Back to home</a>
      <div class="big" style="margin-top:22px">🧗</div>
      <h1>Bouldering</h1>
      <p class="lede">No ropes. No harness. Just you, the wall, and a problem to solve. Bouldering is chess for your body — and the beta doesn't always come from where you expect.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="eyebrow">The sport</div>
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:12px">What bouldering actually is</h2>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-bottom:36px">Bouldering is climbing on short walls — usually 4–5 metres — without ropes or harnesses, with a crash pad below. Each route is called a "problem," and problems are graded by difficulty. The goal is to reach the top using only the holds marked for that problem.</p>
      <div class="feat">
        <article><div class="n">Movement</div><h3>Full-body problem solving</h3><p>Every problem is a sequence of moves — footwork, body positioning, tension, timing. Getting it wrong at move seven means starting over. Getting it right feels like cracking a code.</p></article>
        <article><div class="n">Mental game</div><h3>The "beta"</h3><p>Beta is the sequence of moves used to solve a problem. You can try your own beta or learn from others — but sometimes the obvious solution isn't the right one, and you have to see the wall differently to progress.</p></article>
        <article><div class="n">Physical</div><h3>Strength meets precision</h3><p>Raw grip strength gets you started. Technique — silent feet, hip placement, reading your center of gravity — takes you further. The balance between brute force and finesse shifts as grades go up.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tight" style="background:var(--paper-2)">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="two">
        <div>
          <div class="eyebrow">The grade</div>
          <h2 style="margin-bottom:14px">V-scale progression</h2>
          <p class="lede">Problems run from V0 (beginner) to V17 (world-class elite). Each grade jump is a genuine step up in body tension, contact strength, and spatial problem-solving. The progression is addictive.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pills" style="align-content:start"><span>Indoor bouldering</span><span>Footwork & technique</span><span>Overhang problems</span><span>Dynamic moves</span><span>Slab climbing</span><span>Project sessions</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="eyebrow">Why I climb</div>
      <div class="feat">
        <article><div class="n">Presence by necessity</div><h3>Entirely in the wall</h3><p>Most activities let your mind wander. Bouldering doesn't. When you're five moves into a hard problem, you're not thinking about markets or code — you're entirely in the wall. That forced presence is part of why it works as a counterweight to desk-heavy work.</p></article>
        <article><div class="n">The reset</div><h3>Physical and mental</h3><p>An hour of climbing drains a different kind of battery than a day of intellectual work. You leave a session physically spent but mentally clear — which is a rare combination to find in a single activity.</p></article>
        <article><div class="n">The habit</div><h3>Problems that wait for you</h3><p>A problem you couldn't finish today will still be there next week. Coming back to something with fresh eyes — after sleep, after thinking about it — and suddenly seeing the solution is a feeling that never gets old.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
